[proved in Section <<ref:s_pr1>>] The closure graph $G_2$ for congruence classes of\/ $2\times 2$ matrices is given in Figure <<ref:kib5>>. \begin{figure}[hbt]

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\xymatrix@R=7pt@C=8pt{
{\begin{bmatrix} 0&-1\\ 1&1
\end{bmatrix}}
&
{\begin{bmatrix} 0&1\\ \lambda &0
\end{bmatrix}}
&{\begin{bmatrix} 1&\\
&1 \end{bmatrix}}
&
*[r]{\dim\ 3}
\\ \\
&{\begin{bmatrix}
1&\\ &0
\end{bmatrix}}\ar[uu] \ar[luu]
\ar[uur]&
&
*[r]{\dim\ 2}
\\
{\begin{bmatrix} 0&1\\
-1&0
\end{bmatrix}}\ar[uuu]& & &
*[r]{\dim\ 1}
\\
&{\begin{bmatrix} 0&\\
&0
\end{bmatrix}}
\ar[ul]\ar[uu] & &
*[r]{\dim\ 0}
} \end{split}
\end{equation}

\end{figure} Each congruence class is represented by its canonical matrix, which is a direct sum of blocks of the form <<ref:can>> $($the zero entries outside of these blocks are not shown$)$. The graph is infinite: $\left[\begin{smallmatrix} 0&1\\
\lambda &0
\end{smallmatrix}\right]$ represents the infinite set of vertices indexed by $\lambda
\in\mathbb C \smallsetminus\{-1,1\}$ $($provided that each nonzero $\lambda$ is determined up to replacement by $\lambda ^{-1})$ with arrows $\left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1&\\ &0
\end{smallmatrix}\right]\to
\left[\begin{smallmatrix} 0&1\\
\lambda &0
\end{smallmatrix}\right]$ to each of these vertices. The congruence classes of canonical matrices located at the same horizontal level in $G_2$ have the same dimension, which is indicated to the right.
